 "summary": "A second-order (many-sorted) logical language for representing dynamically changing worlds: actions, situations (histories of actions built from S0 by do(a,s)) and fluents, with Reiter's successor-state axioms solving the frame problem; the foundation of Golog and much of cognitive robotics.",
 "status": "theoretical",
 "defined_by": [
  "academic-paper"
 ],
 "level_notes": {
  "1": "McCarthy 1963 and McCarthy & Hayes 1969 introduce it; Reiter's 1991 paper and book give the standard axiomatisation.",
  "0": "Theoretical formalism; executable via Golog interpreters (see golog).",
  "2": "Secondary coverage is textbook chapters and the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy entries (filed at level 3)."
 },
